EXECUTION/SECURITY AUDIT
Lumen calls upstream Bifrost typed request/response and stream converters directly.
It deliberately does not instantiate the Bifrost orchestration core or provider HTTP
clients. The core contains key rotation, fallback, and encrypted-reasoning repair
attempts. Provider utils.ConfigureDialer installs StaleConnectionRetryIfErr and
BuildStreamingClient copies MaxIdemponentCallAttempts/RetryIfErr, so setting only
max_retries=0 is insufficient. This runtime's HTTP/1 transport performs a single
RoundTrip, using a fresh connection, no proxy, no redirects, no GetBody, no
idempotency headers, verified TLS, and request context cancellation. No provider SDK
HTTP retry policy, credential discovery chain, key selection, tools, database,
response cache, model pricing, or upstream payload/error logger is installed.

Native same-wire Responses, Anthropic Messages/count_tokens, and OpenAI-compatible
embedding payloads/events use bounded raw transport to preserve fields not present
in upstream typed schemas (including signed thinking and compaction). Cross-wire
chat/Responses/Messages and Gemini/Bedrock embedding/event conversion use upstream
Bifrost converters, not a nominal proxy. Provider cost estimates are not authority.

CLOUD AUTHENTICATION BOUNDARIES
Azure uses a request-local API key or explicit Authorization Bearer header and an
administrator-owned API base. Vertex uses an explicit bearer token and full
publisher/model base, or a request-local API key at a compatible custom endpoint;
no ambient service-account discovery or refresh is claimed. Bedrock accepts a
request-local bearer token plus explicit API base, or explicit JSON key material
(region, access_key_id, secret_access_key, optional session_token), signed once with
AWS SigV4. SDK default AWS/GCP chains are intentionally not available. ChatGPT/Codex
subscription is owned by Lumen's strict-target Python transport, not this runtime.

SOURCE-LEVEL RISKS / ACCEPTANCE LIMITS
Upstream typed conversion is model-aware and can normalize unsupported fields at
conversion time. Native same-wire raw paths avoid that loss; cross-wire protocols
cannot promise round-trip equivalence for arbitrary future vendor extensions.
Cloud service-account/STS refresh and provider account management belong outside
this process. Synthetic tests do not prove live provider credentials, service terms,
cloud SDK feature parity, invoice accuracy, or multi-architecture deployment.
